hi this is damon root for a reason  television i'm here today with josh  Blackman professor of law at South Texas  College of Law in Houston and author of  the new book unprecedented it's the  constitutional challenge to Obamacare  now obviously is over the over the two  years as the legal challenge to the  health care law was was working its way  through the courts was before the  American people that the word  unprecedented was thrown around a lot by  the Challenger it's it's an  unprecedented exercise of federal power  it's an unprecedented attack on on the  on the individual citizen what did what  made you choose this title for the book  well the Obamacare law was very much a  first in several respects it was  unprecedented in that never before had a  lob in past such magnitude that any  Republican volume it was impressive in  the fact that it never before had  Congress for people do stuff I was also  unprecedented in the way which was  passed kind of ramped through the last  minute without deliberation Randy  Barnett a law professor at George John  who was when the leading architect of  this challenge kept using this word over  and over again say that this law is  unprecedented that Congress had never  done this before and at an event I made  an offhand comment to my blog saying hey  we should have a Randy Barnett drinking  game we know we take a shot whenever  randy said unprecedented and if we did  that we'd all be drunk on the floor  please at every five minutes and Randy  actually said later that that  crystallized in his mind the power of  that word they did that Congress is  doing something that had never done  before has a lot of rhetorical force and  less people understand how grave this  law is so in deciding in the book this  was definitely the right title because  this was an unprecedented case you talk  about how how the case well first the  health care law itself and the way it  was passed and then the legal challenge  is it gathers steam it creates this sort  of constitutional moment in America and  and and it and it and it is energizes  tea party activists it energizes people  on both sides of the debate terms  talking about the Constitution that's  not unprecedented American history but I  mean you make a very strong argument  that that is one of these moments that  we're going to look back and see that  this is when the Constitution and  debates about our system are really at  the forefront right i mean the period  from 2009 the 2012 was remarkable  the first time you had a strong  constitutional movement pop up out of  nowhere the Tea Party this wasn't  organized this wasn't happening kind of  a a founding cause it was just people  who had a dissatisfaction with the scope  of the federal government in their power  and at just the right moment President  Obama gave him their exact gift  Obamacare and this was like the fuel for  their movement and people weren't  objecting to this law purely on policy  grounds I mean they didn't like it was  going to waste money and it was bad  policy but they said this is  unconstitutional the Constitution does  not give Congress this power and  rallying behind Obamacare their tea  party grew magnificently and we saw in  2010 the republicans gained so many  seats in the House largely in the backs  of voting against people supported obama  care and in 2012 it kind of faded away  because by that point the supreme court  had already upheld the the law so so so  the Obamacare law actually kind of  represents a full art of the rise and  slow descent of the Tea Party I would  pause say that I think that the Tea  Party movement is lying dormant it's  lined or because the sentiments against  this expansion are still existing it  just would require the right trigger to  bring it back and i think one aspect of  the obamacare case is a federal  government should be leery about what  kinds of exertions they make people like  nancy pelosi thought there was no  problem with this when people said is  this constitution said are you kidding  me i don't think people in the democrats  particular appreciated how divisive such  an issue was and they pay the price for  it going forward I think we'll see  Congress maybe not being so keen to pass  laws such a large scale out of fear of  what will happen let me let's let's talk  about the Chief Justice John Roberts and  his opinion and and and the arguments  are though the reasoning that goes into  it and you really have a very thorough  discussion I mean I really have the  whole case from from from every aspect  and it's and it's and it's also I should  just I just like to add I mean it's you  it's incredibly gripping account I mean  this is you have a you have a minute mad  minute break down second-by-second  granny form of yet like that's right  lecture 24 of the of the decision day as  it comes down but the chief justice he  I mean as we all know he upheld the law  heap sort of formed this coalition with  with the Liberals on the court to do so  but but you you you and you make this  point several times in the book that he  rewrote the law you say he upheld a  statute that Congress did not write I  mean can you explain what do you mean by  that ok so when Obamacare was passed in  2009 we have to remember the president's  campaign pledge in 2008 the president  said I will not raise taxes that was his  pledge so in framing the Obamacare law  they were very deliberate the way the  Obamacare law works is that if you don't  have health insurance you have to pay  this penalty and the statute called it  just that a penalty it wasn't called  attacks but as the litigation proceeded  one of the arguments that government you  use to support it was saying listen this  is just a tax we are just taxing people  I'm not having health insurance but the  statute didn't say that the statute  called it a penalty was very clear so in  order for the Chief Justice to uphold  the statute he had like make-believe  time like mr. Rogers he said well even  though it says penalty we're going to  pretend you know I make believe that it  says tax because if it's said tax then  we could uphold it piece of cake no  problem so John Roberts in his opinion  called a saving construction upheld the  law that Congress never wrote this is a  law that Congress could have never  passed in 2010 because have they called  it a tax they have gone slammed at the  polls and in the public opinion this law  was passed without calling it attacks  and I was upheld by calling it attacks  the response to the decision you know I  would group it into two broad categories  there's sort of a political partisan  response if you like the law you're  happy with the lobbying upheld if you  didn't like it you're unhappy about it  but then you look at the response among  legal experts law professors legal  scholars it was it's more complicated  actually more interesting where you had  liberal legal academics very prominent  ones who basically supported the law  thought Congress had the power to enact  the the individual mandate under the  Commerce Clause they were quite upset  with Roberts ruling because he rejected  that argument and for conservatives did  and then you have sort of libertarian  conservative law professors challenging  law they actually see a sort of a  victory for some of their ideas  are they let me put it to you this way  are the are these liberals right to be a  little upset at the outcome of the case  another libertarians a little right to  be to be cheered by the outcome Damon  that's exactly right when this case for  start in 2009 is a laugher I'm I talk  about in my book how at the 2009 federal  Saudi convention in Washington right  here Randy Barnett myself and a few  other lawyers were just talking about  this case and at the time no one thought  there was any way the Supreme Court  would ever have a single vote in the  other than justice thomas to strike this  down but fast forward two and a half  years and this argument gained steam and  gained traction and started to be  accepted by judges and some scholars and  then we go to the Supreme Court where  five justices say that Congress can't  regulate inactivity seven justices say  that the federal government can't coerce  States into accepting this Medicaid  money holy cow we can put aside what  this means for the future and what what  doctrinally this means but the court  made it very clear that the so-called  federalism revolution of the rehnquist  Court is still ongoing and that the  court will police the bounds before the  federal government can and cannot do and  this opinion makes very clear that the  structural protections of our  Constitution the doctrine of enumerated  powers like the Commerce Clause of  federalism between the states and the  federal government those matter and they  don't just matter in the abstract those  are the key bowl works in protecting our  freedoms and our individual liberty and  the fact that you had five votes for  that I think is really significant and  it should represent a shift in the way  people approach this so I said before  that perhaps the governor might be Lee  or even passing new laws this your  factor into it because you now have the  strong constitutional under current in  the people about what the federal  government can do you have the Supreme  Court on record saying there's certain  things government can and can't do and  this actually is not so bad for  libertarians this actually might be a  good recognition that the Constitution  has very strong libertarian principles  embedded into it there are still some  big challenges in the courts towards the  law what do you make of the prospects of  any of those so there are a couple  lingering challenges one involves the  so-called contraceptive  mandate this law says that employers  have to provide contraceptives and  abortion procedures to their employees  and the argument goes well my business  is a Christian business i running a  craft store and i run it with a  Christian virtue or on all my employees  to be devoted to christ and it goes  against my religious beliefs well it's  not really your believes it's a beliefs  of your corporation but the court has  held that corporations do have certain  constitutional rights and within the  next year the court will consider one of  those cases of whether the government  can force a Christian oriented or Jewish  oriented business to offer these pills  or these products it's a tough call  another challenge involved a so-called  Medicaid exchanges because this law was  passed so hastily there are a lot of  things that didn't really make sense and  the way the laws were in now if any  state's decide not to participate in  Obamacare the people in those states are  not allowed to get subsidies for the  health insurance HHS said now we're  going to it anyway and they're probably  breaking the law and those cases should  resolve the next few months their number  of lingering cases but I think  ultimately the Chief Justice decision  saved Obamacare I'm not too convinced  that any of these repeal or defund  efforts will work no matter how many  commercials we see for it because this  law is a law of the land we're kind of  stuck with it that might be a bad thing  I'd be a good thing but going forward i  think the lesson we have to take from  this is how the constitutional element  progressed so quickly and flourished and  when we saw that the Constitution when  the federal government went too far and  what they did the American people clawed  back and I think we will keep that ethos  with us going forward as we develop the  Constitution and in response to the  growing power of the federal government  thank you very much Josh Blackman author  of the new book unprecedented the  constitutional challenge to Obamacare  thank you so much Jimmy thank you for  recent television I'm David rhew  you

